    "data": {
      "title": "Bit Commands",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": false,
      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        unsigned int number, bit;\n        char command[7];\n        scanf(\"%u %u %6s\", &number, &bit, command);\n        unsigned int mask = 1U << bit;\n\n        if (strcmp(command, \"ON\") == 0) {\n            number |= mask;\n        } else if (strcmp(command, \"OFF\") == 0) {\n            number &= ~mask;\n        } else {\n            number ^= mask;\n        }\n        printf(\"%u\\n\", number);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}",
      "statement": "Modify bit [[bit]] of [[number]]: {{ON}} sets it to {{1}}, {{OFF}} sets it to {{0}}, and {{TOGGLE}} flips it.\n\nBits are numbered from {{0}} on the right (the least significant bit). Each command is independent. Print each resulting number in decimal.",
